+# Batch 67 authoring notes
+
+## Scope and plan reconciliation
+
+- Owned A/B pair: `qftx-gaga-projective-scalar-dolbeault` and `qftx-gaga-projective-scalar-dolbeault-examples`; category/library `quantum-field-theory-mathematics` / `mathematics`.
+- No separate design section governs this pair. Scaffold from the current plan, the owner direction, and the inspected sources; preserve the full scalar comparison claim and the two assigned examples.
+- The dispatch lists eight A-page `requires` pages. The current `research/plan-spec.json` and batch-67 manifest list those eight plus `hormander-estimates-and-the-levi-problem` and `compact-laplace-type-operators-and-hodge-representatives`. Preserve the current plan's ten-page interface; record this dispatch/plan conflict for the owner.
+- The plan's theorem listed 12 direct external analytic dependencies and item level 1; the examples each listed the theorem and level 2. The full batch graph records batch 67 at level 0 (no in-run page suppliers). The proof is now split into the missing projective twisting-bundle definition (level 0), the Laurent/Čech supplier (level 1), the general vector-bundle Dolbeault theorem (level 2), and the examples (level 3). The theorem retains its general planned claim and now depends on the local Laurent/Čech supplier; the analytic inputs used only by that supplier are recorded there rather than redundantly as theorem edges. The examples depend on both the theorem and the Laurent/Čech supplier because they display the explicit contraction. Recompute and validate all item levels after final edits; do not edit the shared plan.
+- The local A and B page carriers and three planned item carriers were absent at inspection. The target Step-1 record was also absent (`loadStep1Record` + `step1Decision`: `Readiness record missing`). This was a missing readiness artifact, not a mathematical gap. The five local item carriers and two frontmatter-only pages now exist; readiness records remain pending the final contracts and scoped checks.
+
+## Supplier and interface inspection
+
+The 12 planned external item suppliers were read at their imported physics snapshots. Their raw hashes match `research/math-imports.json` and the originals in `/home/lazyinspirit/Projects/prestige-math-library`.
+
+- `def-axiom-of-choice`: every family of nonempty sets has a choice function; the local comparison argument is conditional on full AC.
+- `thm-dolbeault-lemma-polydisc`: full AC; a smooth closed $(p,q)$-form on a polydisc has a primitive on a coordinatewise compactly contained smaller polydisc.
+- `thm-dolbeault-cohomology-polydisc-vanishes-positive-q`: full AC; every positive-$q$ closed smooth form on a product of finite discs and/or $C$ is exact on that whole product.
+- `thm-smooth-function-module-sheaves-are-acyclic`: full AC; every sheaf of real-smooth-function modules is acyclic on every open subset. Its current `verification.precheck` is `pending`, while its inspected proof and audited published carrier give the required interface; pending precheck alone is not a gap.
+- `thm-acyclic-resolution-theorem-for-right-derived-functors`: DC; an exact resolution by $F$-acyclic terms computes right derived functors when every cycle object lies in the supplied injective-data domain.
+- `thm-cauchy-integral-formula-on-a-polydisc`: iterated Cauchy integrals for continuous separately holomorphic functions; the source and carrier expressly do not interchange contour integrals.
+- `thm-laurent-expansion-annulus`: unique Laurent coefficients and local uniform convergence on each annulus.
+- `lem-holomorphic-dependence-of-slice-laurent-coefficients`: coefficient holomorphy for the stated two-variable Hartogs figure only; do not silently enlarge that interface to an arbitrary product.
+- `thm-locally-uniform-limit-of-holomorphic-functions-in-several-variables`: local uniform limits and all derivatives converge locally uniformly and remain holomorphic.
+- `thm-leray-acyclic-cover-theorem`: full AC and an ordered open cover whose every nonempty finite intersection is acyclic for the coefficient sheaf.
+- `thm-cech-to-sheaf-cohomology-comparison`: full AC; it defines the fixed-cover canonical comparison from the Čech–Godement double complex.
+- `cor-dolbeault-vanishing-pseudoconvex-domain`: full AC; for a Hartogs-pseudoconvex domain in $C^n$, $1\le q\le n$, every smooth closed $(0,q)$-form is globally $\bar\partial$-exact. Its separate weighted-$L^2$ assertion is not used. The inspected proof constructs a smooth strictly plurisubharmonic weight and invokes the actual smooth-data branch of the imported Hörmander result.
+
+Additional inspected definitions used to prove the exact projective-intersection hypothesis are `def-polydisc-boundary-radius`, `def-plurisubharmonic-function`, and `def-plurisubharmonic-exhaustion-and-hartogs-pseudoconvexity`. For an intersection $U_I\cong\mathbb C^a\times(\mathbb C^*)^b$, its equal-radius boundary function is $\min_j|w_j|$ over its nonzero coordinates, so $-\log\delta=\max_j(-\log|w_j|)$. Each term is harmonic on $\mathbb C^*$, and the finite maximum is plurisubharmonic by the line-submean definition. The case $b=0$ is the whole-space convention. Thus the pseudoconvex-vanishing supplier applies only after this calculation.
+
+**Planned-supplier interface correction.** `lem-holomorphic-dependence-of-slice-laurent-coefficients` is not in the authored lemma's `deps`: its inspected hypothesis requires a function on the Hartogs figure $H(r,s)$, whose thin cylinder contains the annular coordinate value $0$ for small parameter values. The standard intersections exclude $0$ in each punctured coordinate for every parameter, so that figure is not contained in them. The item's claim is therefore not a premise for this use. The complete proof instead derives parameter holomorphy by iterated Cauchy integrals on compact contour products. This removes one unsupported planned edge; no imported item was edited or escalated.
+
+## Source reading and retrieval
+
+- Jiří Lebl, *Tasty Bits of Several Complex Variables*, v4.4, https://www.jirka.org/scv/scv.pdf. Read §1.1 Theorem 1.1.4, §1.2, §2.5, and §4.4 Lemma 4.4.7 / Theorem 4.4.5 (printed pp. 15–16, 20–23, 90–101, 142–145). The local Dolbeault argument is detailed. The proposed several-variable Laurent expansion in Exercise 1.2.4, the q=1 reduction in Exercise 4.4.6, and the coefficient holomorphy check in Exercise 4.4.14 are prompts, not proofs; the current local proof supplies those arguments.
+- MIT OpenCourseWare 18.117, *Topics in Several Complex Variables*, Spring 2005, Jonathan Campbell notes, https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-117-topics-in-several-complex-variables-spring-2005/3e8b0c3499d6226959485ace042cdaab_18117notes.pdf. Read lectures 4, 7–9, and 11 (PDF pp. 9–12, 18–23, 23–25). This supplies an independent projective-atlas and Čech/Dolbeault treatment; its last comparison proof is a sketch and invokes pseudoconvex $\bar\partial$ solvability without proving it. A displayed chart condition appears transcribed as $Z_i=0$, but the chart map divides by $Z_i$; the intended standard chart is $Z_i\ne0$.
